s variants of an enumerated type, but the diagrams are separate. The instruction says \"Each distinct packet-format ASCII diagram must be represented as a separate entry in 'packet'.\" So we have separate entries. We can still define an enumerated type for opcode within each entry? Or we can define a common enumerated type? The schema allows each packet entry to have its own enumerated_types. We can define an enumerated type for opcode in each entry, but that would be redundant. Alternatively, we can define a single enumerated type in one entry and reference it? But the schema doesn't have cross-references. The instruction says \"All extracted information associated with that diagram, including PDUs, bit string types, enumerated types, structure types, array types, parsing context, helper functions, and transform functions, must be stored only within that diagram's entry.\" So each entry is self-contained. So we can define an enumerated type for opcode in each entry, but that would be repetitive. However, we can also define a bit string type for opcode and not enumerate. The RFC lists opcodes in a table. We can represent that as an enumerated type. But we need to decide.\n\nLet's analyze each packet:\n\n1. RRQ/WRQ packet (Figure 5-1): Fields: Opcode (2 bytes), Filename (string, variable length, terminated by zero byte), 0 (1 byte), Mode (string, variable length, terminated by zero byte), 0 (1 byte). Actually the diagram shows: 2 bytes, string, 1 byte, string, 1 byte. So the structure is: Opcode (2 bytes), Filename (string), zero byte (1 byte), Mode (string), zero byte (1 byte). The zero bytes are terminators. So we have fields: opcode, filename, zero_byte_1, mode, zero_byte_2. But the zero bytes are part of the string representation? The RFC says \"The file name is a sequence of bytes in netascii terminated by a zero byte.\" So the zero byte is part of the filename field? In the diagram, it's separate. We can represent it as a separate field. But we need to be careful about sizes. The filename is variable length, so size_bits is null. The zero byte is 1 byte. The mode is variable length, terminated by zero byte. So we have two variable-length strings and two zero bytes. We can represent the zero bytes as separate fields. Or we can represent the filename as a string with a terminator. The schema doesn't have a string type, but we can use a bit string type with size null. We'll define a structure type for RRQ/WRQ.\n\n2. DATA packet (Figure 5-2): Fields: Opcode (2 bytes), Block # (2 bytes), Data (n bytes). Data length is variable, from 0 to 512 bytes. So we have opcode, block_number, data. Data size is variable, we can represent as \"n\" or expression? The RFC says \"The data field is from zero to 512 bytes long.\" So we can set size_bits to null and constraints like \"data_length <= 512\" but we don't have a length field. We can represent data as an array of bytes? Or as a bit string with size null. We'll define a structure type.\n\n3. ACK packet (Figure 5-3): Fields: Opcode (2 bytes), Block # (2 bytes). So fixed size.\n\n4. ERROR packet (Figure 5-4): Fields: Opcode (2 bytes), ErrorCode (2 bytes), ErrMsg (string, variable length, terminated by zero byte), 0 (1 byte). So similar to RRQ/WRQ.\n\n5. UDP header (appendix): Fields: Source Port (2 bytes), Destination Port (2 bytes), Length (2 bytes), Checksum (2 bytes). Fixed size.\n\nNow, we need to define bit string types for each field. For example, opcode is a 16-bit integer. We can define a bit string type \"opcode\" with size 16. But we also have enumerated type for opcode. We can define an enumerated type \"opcode\" with variants for each value. But the schema for enumerated_types has \"variants\" with \"name\", \"type\", \"condition\". The \"type\" might be the value? Actually the schema says: \"variants\": [ { \"name\": \"string\", \"type\": \"string\", \"condition\": \"string or null\" } ]. So we can define variants like \"RRQ\" with type \"1\" or condition? The instruction s